-- title:
--   Inertia-fixed norm-free classes lie in the specialisation domain
-- statement:
--   The ambient arithmetic data are: a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a prime $p$, a natural number $M$ with $5\le M$ and $p\nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb Q$ of type $\{p\}$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta\in L$, and an intermediate field $K$ of $L\subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ required by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb Q$. Further, $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and with $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with the tower $A\subseteq L\subseteq K$ is fixed, and $j$ is a nonzero element of $K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficient extension to $L$ of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`). The residue field $\kappa=$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField ↥Pl` is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$ and is an $A$-algebra; `hPl` says that $p$ lies in the non-units of $Pl$, `ρ` is a ring homomorphism $A\to Pl$ with `hρ` making it compatible with $A\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and `hAlgκ` says that $A\to\kappa$ is the residue map composed with `ρ`. Algebra structures $A\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $L\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with the tower condition are fixed. Throughout, $X=$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A (↥K) j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) with structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A (↥K) j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) (the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closures `chartAlgFin`, `chartAlgInf` of $A[j]$, $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$), assumed proper over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; `hsmL`, `hgiL` assert that its base change to $L$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral. A `MulSemiringAction` of $L\simeq_{\mathbb Q}L$ on $A$ is fixed, with `hΓA` asserting compatibility of the action with $A\to L$.
--
--   Special fibre: $C_1,C_2$ are schemes proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral over $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$, together with closed immersions $i_1,i_2$ of $C_1,C_2$ into the base change $X_\kappa$ over $\kappa$; `hcover` says every point of $X_\kappa$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$, `hred` that the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` that its cardinality is $n>0$. Sections are given: $\varepsilon$ of $X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$ of $c_1,c_2$, with `hε₁` saying that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $\kappa$; `hεC₂` says the reduction of $\varepsilon$ avoids the image of $i_2$.
--
--   Relative Picard data: $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $X/A$ (a scheme $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec}A$ with a zero section), `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents the rigidified line bundles on $X$ satisfying the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero condition `algEquivZeroCut`, and `hsm`, `hsep` that $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and separated; `hprL`, `hgcL` assert that the second projection of $D.P\times_{\operatorname{Spec}A}\operatorname{Spec}L$ is proper with geometrically connected fibres. Similarly `hreps` is a representability datum for the base change of $D$ to $\kappa$, and `hPk` identifies its Poincaré bundle with the base change of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some`; `hDL`, `hPL` are the analogous datum and identification over $L$. $D_1,D_2$ are designations for $c_1,c_2$ with representability data `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. The morphism $\nu_2$ over $\kappa$ from $D_\kappa$ to $D_2$ satisfies `hν₂`: for every $\kappa$-scheme $T$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $D_\kappa$, the pullback along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂.some` is isomorphic to the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of the pullback along $a$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps`.
--
--   Generic fibre and Abel–Jacobi data: $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), $e_\eta$ an isomorphism of $M_\eta.C$ with $X_{\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`); `hMηpin` says that on the finite chart the identification of the function field of $M_\eta.C$ with the Igusa-style Laurent field carries each element of `chartAlgFin` to the coefficient extension of its Laurent expansion, and `hgal` says that for every $g\in\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ fixing $L$ pointwise and every pair of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of $M_\eta.C$ related by the $g$-twist on the finite chart, the associated places are related by [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) applied to $g$. Over $L$, `ajL` is a morphism from $X_L$ to $D_L$ with `hajLε` sending the section to the zero section and `hajL` asserting the Abel–Jacobi property: for every field $K'$ over $L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of $X_L$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $\varepsilon$. The morphisms `kL` (comparison of the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$- and $L$-base changes, with `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`), `ajbar` (defined by `hajbar` as $e_\eta$ followed by `kL`, `ajL` and the first projection, and lying over the base by `hajbar_over`) and the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point `εbar` of $M_\eta.C$ (with `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj` expressing that it is the point $\varepsilon$ and is sent to the zero section) are fixed; `hpts_aj` asserts that for $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x,s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ equal to $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[\,\text{place of }x\,]-[\,\text{place of }s\,]$ whose class corresponds under `gpts` to $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   Points, Hecke and Galois actions: `gpts` is a bijection from $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})=$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`, onto the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of $D$; $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) an endomorphism of $D$ over $A$, and $\tau$ assigns to each $s\in L\simeq_{\mathbb Q}L$ a semilinear endomorphism of $D$ over the twist of the base by $s$. The hypotheses `hφmul` (each $\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some`), `hφpts` ($\varphi$ computes the Hecke action of `heckeModuleOneBar` on points through `gpts`), `hτ1`, `hτmul`, `hτφ` ($\tau$ is multiplicative and commutes with the $\varphi(t)$), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive) and `hτpts` (`gpts` intertwines the Galois action on $J_1(Mp)$ with $\tau$) govern these data; `hin` and `hcomm` are the Hecke–diamond input and commutation hypotheses [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) and [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54).
--
--   Special fibre operator datum: $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), consisting of groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}$ and a surjection $G.\mathrm{proj}:G.J0s\to G.JI\times G.JE$ with kernel the torus; `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` are bijections of $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with the $\kappa$-points of $D_\kappa$, $D_1$, $D_2$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that the corresponding Poincaré pullbacks are multiplicative in each variable up to isomorphism, `hproj` that $G.\mathrm{proj}$ is computed by post-composition with `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁` and with $\nu_2$, and `hPTS` that `pts` is a homomorphism for the relative group law of `hreps`. $O$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePOpsV3.html#L13) (Hecke operators on $J0s$, $JI$, $JE$, diamond operators `diamondP`, `diamondN`, the involution $w$, the inertia action, `verI`, `frobE`, `diamondNI`, with the commutation and torus-triviality axioms), and `hO` has four clauses: for each prime $\ell$ the operator $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,\ell$ corresponds under `pts` to $\varphi$ of `heckeGenOne ℓ`; for $b\in(\mathbb Z/p)^\times$ and $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d\equiv1\pmod M$ and $d\equiv b\pmod p$, $O.\mathrm{diamondP}\,b$ corresponds to $\varphi$ of `diamondGen d`; for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d\equiv1\pmod p$, $O.\mathrm{diamondN}\,d$ corresponds to $\varphi$ of `diamondGen d`; and for $b$ and $s\in L\simeq_{\mathbb Q}L$ with $s\zeta=\zeta^{b}$, $O.\mathrm{inertia}\,b$ corresponds to $\tau(s)$.
--
--   Integral points over inertia-fixed rings and the good-reduction part: `hF3` is the $\mathcal O_I$-points theorem. For every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ contained in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, fixing every $p$-th root of unity, and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, writing $\mathcal O_I=Pl\cap\overline{\mathbb Q}^{\,I}$, it provides a ring homomorphism $\rho_I:A\to\mathcal O_I$ lifting $A\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ such that, with $\mathrm{dom}$ the set of $x\in J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ whose point `gpts x` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_I$: every element of $\mathrm{dom}$ is fixed by $I$; two $\mathcal O_I$-points of $D$ with the same $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-restriction coincide; $0\in\mathrm{dom}$ and $\mathrm{dom}$ is closed under differences; for every $n>0$ prime to $p$, reduction to $\kappa$ is injective on $n$-torsion $\mathcal O_I$-points and every $n$-torsion $\kappa$-point lifts to an $n$-torsion $\mathcal O_I$-point; and $\mathrm{dom}$ is stable under any Frobenius element at $p$ normalising $I$. Next, $\mathcal A$ is a scheme over $\operatorname{Spec}A$ with a closed immersion $\iota$ into $D.P$ over $A$, and `h𝒜` asserts: $\iota$ is a closed immersion; $\mathcal A\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ is proper and smooth with connected geometric fibres; the image of $\mathcal A$-points is functorially a subgroup of the points of $D$ (containing the unit, closed under multiplication and inversion for the relative group law of `hrep.some`); the subgroup [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) (the range of the endomorphism `normFreeEnd` at the chosen representatives at $p$) consists exactly of those $x$ for which `gpts x` factors through $\mathcal A$; and the image of $\mathcal A$-points is stable under each $\varphi(t)$. Finally `hF10` asserts that for every $\kappa$-algebra $T'$ and every $T'$-point $v$ of $D_\kappa$ which lifts to $\mathcal A$, composing $v$ with $\varphi$ of $\sum_{b\in(\mathbb Z/p)^\times}$ `diamondGen` of the Chinese-remainder lift of $(b,1)$ gives the unit point.
--
--   Igusa components: $w$ is an `IntegralWeightOneForm` over $\kappa$ of level $M$, and $\mathrm{Mdl}_1,\mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $\kappa$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), with isomorphisms $e_1:\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C\cong C_1$ and $e_2:\mathrm{Mdl}_2.C\cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`). The hypothesis `hgauss₁` identifies, for every element $a$ of `chartAlgFin` and all power series $x,y$ over $A$ with $\bar y\neq0$ and $a\cdot y=x$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, the Laurent expansion of the corresponding element of the Igusa function field with $\bar x/\bar y$ over $\kappa$; `hεgal` asserts that $\varepsilon$ is equivariant for any automorphism of the two-chart model over the twist by $s\in L\simeq_{\mathbb Q}L$ that is induced on the finite chart by a coefficientwise twisting automorphism. A finite set `nodesIg` of pairs of places of the Igusa function field is given, together with isomorphisms $\Psi:G.J0s\cong$ `GluedPic0 κ (igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w) nodesIg` and $\theta_1,\theta_2$ of $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of that field; `hF4c` has seven clauses: `nodesIg` consists exactly of the pairs of places coming from $\kappa$-points of the intersection of $C_1$ and $C_2$; its cardinality is $n$; each of its two projections is injective on it; $\Psi$ is compatible with $G.\mathrm{proj}$ and $(\theta_1,\theta_2)$ via `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair`; the image of $G.\mathrm{torus}$ under $\Psi$ is the range of `GluedPic0.nodeUnit`; and $\theta_1$, $\theta_2$ compute classes of divisors $[x]-[\varepsilon_i]$ on the two components whenever the corresponding Poincaré pullback is isomorphic to the line bundle of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of $\varepsilon_i$. A semilinear automorphism `frobT` of the Igusa function field over $\kappa$ is given which, by `hfrobT`, raises each Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power.
--
--   The specialisation family: for every admissible $I$ (contained in the inertia subgroup at $Pl$, fixing all $p$-th roots of unity, of finite index in that inertia subgroup), `dom𝒟` provides an additive subgroup $\mathcal D_I$ of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ and `sp𝒟` an additive map from $\mathcal D_I$ to `GluedPic0 κ (igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w) nodesIg`, subject to the two-clause interface `hIFm`: membership $y\in\mathcal D_I$ holds if and only if the point `gpts y` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_I$ along the homomorphism chosen by `hF3`; and whenever $y\in\mathcal D_I$ is witnessed by such an $\mathcal O_I$-point $z$ and $u$ is a $\kappa$-point of $D_\kappa$ whose composite with the first projection is the reduction of $z$, then $\mathrm{sp}_I(y)=\Psi(\mathrm{pts}^{-1}(u))$. The hypothesis `hlev` has two clauses: for admissible $J\le I$, every $\mathcal O_I$-point witnessing $y$ extends to an $\mathcal O_J$-point with the same restriction to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and the same reduction to $\kappa$; and, correspondingly, $\mathcal D_I\subseteq\mathcal D_J$ with $\mathrm{sp}_J=\mathrm{sp}_I$ on $\mathcal D_I$.
--
--   Under all of these hypotheses the conclusion is: for every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ with $I\le$ `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, such that every $\sigma\in I$ fixes every $\zeta\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\zeta^p=1$, and such that $I$ has finite index in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, every $y$ in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) satisfying $\sigma\cdot y=y$ for all $\sigma\in I$ belongs to the subgroup $\mathcal D_I$ = `dom𝒟 I hI hIμ hIf`.
--
--   This is the domain clause of the specialisation family attached to the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$: it says that the family's domain subgroups $\mathcal D_I$ contain all inertia-fixed classes in the norm-free part, which is what makes the specialisation map available on the classes that arise in the level-lowering argument at $p$. It is obtained from the extension theorem for inertia-fixed norm-free points over $\mathcal O_I$ together with the membership clause of the interface, and is consumed by the assembly of the $q$-expansion semistable specialisation package with diamond operators.
